# Analysis package — education expenditure vs per-capita income (SSF vs LMC)

Run: `python3 analysis/analysis.py`  (full console report saved in `analysis/run_log.txt`)

| file | contents |
|---|---|
| `analysis.py` | reshape + all estimators + diagnostics; single reproducible script |
| `panel_long.csv` | 492 rows: group × series × year × value (tidy) |
| `panel_wide.csv` | 82 rows: group × year, 6 variables (panel) |
| `results_summary.csv` | 111 rows: every coefficient with coef, se, 95% CI, p, n, df, R², DW, SE type, window, role |
| `diagnostics.csv` | coverage, lag-1 autocorrelation, levels-vs-differenced-vs-detrended correlations, long-span descriptives |
| `headline_numbers.csv` | the group-comparison numbers quoted in the hypothesis |
| `HYPOTHESIS.md` | final hypothesis + evidence table + spuriousness diagnostic + limitations |

## Key structural facts driving the design
- 12 rows = 2 **group aggregates** (SSF, LMC) × 6 series; **no country-level rows** → N=2 units, no FE/GMM.
- `GNI per capita (constant 2015 US$)` only: **LMC 1975–2015 (41 obs), SSF 2011–2015 (5 obs)**.
  No GDP-per-capita series exists → GNIpc is the proxy (documented in the hypothesis).
- `Adjusted savings: education expenditure (% of GNI)` is the only education-funding series; the
  "% of total government expenditure" series named in the dataset description is **absent**.
- LFPR and SSF exports exist only from 1991; 84/492 cells blank; **zero non-numeric junk**; no imputation used.

## Estimation rules applied
Inference uses differenced / lagged specs with Newey–West(4); HAC is used only when n>=20, and whenever HAC
would give a *narrower* interval than classical OLS the wider (classical) interval is reported and flagged in
`se_type`. Models with residual df<8 are labelled `DESCRIPTIVE ONLY` and are never used for inference.
Levels log-log elasticities are reported but explicitly labelled trend-driven colour.
